    <DataNode elementId="AT3G28180-MONOMER" textLabel="Xyloglucan glycosyltransferase 4" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LJP4" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Efforts to identify the Golgi-localized glucan synthase responsible for xyloglucan biosynthesis by traditional methods of protein purification have not been successful |CITS:[6457048][8363577]|. Because of the structural similarity between the &amp;beta;-linked glycan chains of xyloglucan and those of cellulose, the cellulose synthase-like (CSL) genes were suggested as candidates |CITS: [11554468]|, and several results supported this suggestion |CITS: [16574868][19321749][20497371]|. The |FRAME: AT3G28180 CSLC4| gene of |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;| was found based on its homology with the CSLC gene of |FRAME: TAX-4020 &lt;i&gt;Tropaeolum majus&lt;/i&gt;| (common Nasturium). The CSLC gene is highly upregulated during a growth phase during which nasturium accumulates large quantities of xyloglucan as a storage polysaccharide |CITS: [17488821]|. The Arabidopsis |FRAME: AT3G28180 CSLC4| gene was cloned and expressed in the yeast |FRAME: TAX-4922 &lt;i&gt;Pichia pastoris&lt;/i&gt;|. Even though the protein could be detected in |FRAME: TAX-4922 &lt;i&gt;Pichia pastoris&lt;/i&gt;| cell extracts, no &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; activity could be measured. However, cells expressing the gene accumulated soluble &amp;beta;-1,4-linked glucans oligosaccharides of between four and six residues in length. |FRAME: TAX-4922 &lt;i&gt;Pichia pastoris&lt;/i&gt;| cells co-expressing both the |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;| |FRAME: AT3G28180 CSLC4| gene and the |FRAME: AT3G62720 XXT1| gene (encoding |FRAME: AT3G62720-MONOMER xyloglucan 6-xylosyltransferase 1|) accumulated much longer &amp;beta;-1,4-glucans, suggesting that the XT1 and CSLC4 proteins interact directly or indirectly to extend the length of the product |CITS: [17488821]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G28180-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G28180.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9LJP4" />
